WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.220
This is HemoGrid, a blood bank database.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:08.933
The idea is simple: let the database enforce the rules, and keep the web page thin.

00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:16.535
PostgreSQL is the system of record: donors, donations, blood units, requests and allocations.

00:00:16.535 --> 00:00:21.736
MongoDB holds the lab screening reports, whose fields differ for every donor.

00:00:21.736 --> 00:00:26.204
A small web app sits on top, and it only calls database functions.

00:00:27.604 --> 00:00:29.041
First, the E R diagram.

00:00:29.041 --> 00:00:36.351
Person is a superclass, specialised into donor, recipient and staff, because one person can play more than one role.

00:00:36.351 --> 00:00:40.850
A donor's phones and camps are multivalued, so each gets its own table.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:50.284
A donation is separated into blood units, and units are reserved for hospital requests through an allocation table, where a unit can appear only once.

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:52.906
Next, normalization.

00:00:52.906 --> 00:00:57.307
I wrote down the functional dependencies, then decomposed step by step.

00:00:57.307 --> 00:01:04.824
Shelf life depends only on the component, so it lives in its own table, and every table ends up in Boyce Codd normal form.

00:01:04.824 --> 00:01:10.202
If phones and camps shared a table, adding one phone number would force a row per camp.

00:01:10.202 --> 00:01:15.275
That multivalued dependency is why they are split, which gives fourth normal form.

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:21.448
One redundancy is deliberate: a unit's blood group is copied once onto its label, and never changes.

00:01:22.848 --> 00:01:24.159
Now the S Q L objects.

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:27.018
A domain check rejects impossible blood groups.

00:01:27.018 --> 00:01:34.404
A trigger refuses a donation when the donor is under age, deferred, or inside the ninety or one hundred and twenty day gap.

00:01:34.404 --> 00:01:38.514
Another trigger fills in each unit's group, expiry date and barcode.

00:01:38.514 --> 00:01:45.544
A status trigger forbids illegal moves, such as expired back to available, and an audit trigger records every change.

00:01:45.544 --> 00:01:50.905
The allocate units function picks the patient's own group first, then the soonest expiry.

00:01:50.905 --> 00:01:55.551
Views feed the dashboard, and three roles get only the permissions they need.

00:01:56.951 --> 00:01:57.521
Indexing.

00:01:57.521 --> 00:02:10.314
On four hundred thousand rows, the allocation lookup takes about 56 milliseconds as a full scan, but under a tenth of a millisecond with a partial, composite B plus tree on group, component and expiry.

00:02:10.314 --> 00:02:14.874
Barcode scans use a hash index, because they are always an exact match.

00:02:14.874 --> 00:02:19.941
For a date range, only the B plus tree helps, and the planner ignores the hash.

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:22.162
Transactions.

00:02:22.162 --> 00:02:26.771
One O negative unit is left, and two emergency requests arrive together.

00:02:26.771 --> 00:02:33.780
A naive read then write sees the unit as free in both, and only a unique constraint prevents a double booking.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:39.273
With for update skip locked, one request wins and the other stays open, with no error.

00:02:39.273 --> 00:02:43.504
At serializable isolation, one transaction is aborted and retried.

00:02:43.504 --> 00:02:47.987
Locking in opposite order causes a deadlock, which PostgreSQL detects.

00:02:47.987 --> 00:02:55.564
A rollback undoes everything, and a commit is flushed to the write ahead log: atomic, consistent, isolated and durable.

00:02:56.964 --> 00:02:57.362
NoSQL.

00:02:57.362 --> 00:03:03.002
Each screening report is a MongoDB document, and its questionnaire varies per donor.

00:03:03.002 --> 00:03:11.827
PostgreSQL decides pass or fail and creates the units, and the report is written afterwards, so a refusal leaves no orphan document.

00:03:11.827 --> 00:03:17.334
In C A P terms, unit status chooses consistency, while reports can tolerate delay.

00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:23.052
To sum up: constraints, triggers and functions enforce the rules.

00:03:23.052 --> 00:03:26.440
Indexes and locks give speed and safe concurrency.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:30.425
Roles limit access, and MongoDB holds the flexible reports.

00:03:30.425 --> 00:03:34.610
Thirteen database tests and thirteen interface flows cover it.
